\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 In the penthouse gymnasium, Chanta sat on an exercise bike and chugged a beer while a hunk-a-hunk of naked Elvisettes worked out on the equipment for his amusement. The machines that toned up thigh and butt muscles amused him the most. The girls kept getting lovelier, which he put down to youth, or good lighting, but his pleasure with them was waning, as every fantasy he ever had became cinema-grade blockbuster reality then turned to video-release routine.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 For Chanta, even his release was no longer routine as his songwriting went from blockbuster to just blocked. He\'92d written nothing at all in days, apart from the one time he caught himself tracing Sarah\'92s name on Betty Lee\'92s back with a finger, which he stopped doing before she noticed.\
He handed the empty beer glass to an Elvisette he called Marguerita; she took it away with a bottom wiggle that could launch ships to war. Another he called Valerie tried to mount him on the bike, which was not unreasonable since he\'92d perfected a certain exercise with many of them over quite a few repetitions in the past weeks. The trick was setting the seat at the right height (already done), never pedalling too fast, and figuring out who held what handlebar.\
Chanta waved Valerie away, and she too did the ship-launching thing with her butt-wiggling as she went to do some hamstring curls. He was too preoccupied with no ideas that wouldn\'92t fill his head. His thoughts felt as empty as air in a balloon. His song-writing talent hadn\'92t so much dried up as gone into witness protection. It was still out there somewhere, he hoped, under an assumed name, growing a beard, avoiding eye contact, but it had left no forwarding address and would not return his calls.\
What was writer\'92s block anyway? A block was an honest, solid thing, a lump you could shove, or climb, or walk round, or clean up. What he had was anything but solid, just an emptiness that had learned to argue by refusing to say anything.\
Make nothing long enough and you start to think that\'92s the truest thing about you.\
There was never any effort in it before; he would aim himself at a likely subject\'97or rather wait around until a likely subject aimed at him\'97and the lyrics would write themselves. What was waiting around now were fantasies baked into reality going stale, and his waning want for increasingly beautiful women who were not here for him as much as for the experiences of being with a person adjacent to whom they believed he was.\
Things were simpler once. Chanta wondered if he could rewind back to those days, then replay everything that had happened since meeting Sarah in slow motion. So he could find the moment where the thing he didn\'92t say or do could be edited into his life\'97whichever moment it was, whatever the thing had been. Both stayed a size too big for him to get hold of. His happiest moments were when Sarah and he were closest, after the explosion and the hospital, when she came round and tended to his burns. Before Agent Jones broke into his apartment and changed everything, ending all the moments with Sarah he should have cherished more, and would have, if he had known they\'92d be the last moments of their kind. Chanta would rather he remained a burn victim if he could have more of Sarah taking care of him.\
Curiously\'97or perhaps not\'97Chanta never pinned the arc of what his life became to stealing the Gyges Disc from the well-suited but dead Emil Kalheri.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 But he would have ended up inheriting Hardy\'92s riches, and she would\'92ve had that stupid reaction to the painting of Hardy in the penthouse, and he would probably have waved the Elvisettes in Sarah\'92s face before she left. At least, this way he still had a pretty, un-barbecued face.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 As Chanta sat on the exercise bike vacantly eyeing off naked Elvisettes while yearning for simpler times, he thought of the Stickett Inn. He hadn\'92t been back since the bombing. He missed that place, at least as much as he missed the old Memphis Mafia Bar. He missed his old friends who, despite his newfound wealth, he was still not too good for. He checked his watch; Abe and Major would already be there.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Chanta took his piloted hover-copt (he still didn\'92t trust those things to fly themselves), along with three of the most enthusiastic of the Elvisettes\'97who he had to explicitly instruct to get dressed\'97and flew for the few minutes into The Pale to the place where the Stickett had been the centre of his social life.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 He had the pilot circle over the construction site where the inn had been. Basement floors were already poured, and pylons for a shiny new Airborne Tower were reaching at him, already four times taller than the old two-storey shambles had been. By the look of the design of the pylons and support structures, the developer was not even pretending there would be decks built under the Layer where Cloudborne families could squat.\
In the street where he had lain with Sarah before Alexander arrived, shot the place up, and somehow managed to rescue him, Chanta could see tents among rubble still not cleared away, a crowd of people milling around and sitting at tables. He leant on the window trying to recognise anyone in the crowd below, when the street lit up and started shooting at him.\

\f2\i Gunfire!
\f1\i0  That was Chanta\'92s instant reaction. Pops came fast and ragged, little stabs of light stitched up out of smoke, something like a catherine wheel skidded across the bitumen. Chanta did what any reasonable man does when the ground opens fire: he threw himself flat across the seat, wore the last of his beer, and tried to make himself the size of a cushion. God tax it, Halo was right, people 
\f2\i were
\f1\i0  trying to kill him. For a few seconds, he was back, hunched over Sarah under a collapsing ceiling, trying to find the courage to run for the doors.\
\'93Fireworks, sir,\'94 said the pilot, not turning round.\
The three Elvisettes tried not to laugh as Chanta un-flattened himself by degrees. Nothing was worth humiliating this kind of money and, besides, they were genuinely fond of him.\
Chanta looked again, and saw the scene for what it was: an out-for-a-drink-sized crowd in picnic chairs, cheerfully setting fire to things. Smoke rolled flat across the ground. A banner sagged between two tent poles.\
Chanta told the pilot to set them down at the nearest hover-pad stalk, which she did, as other hover-copts held position at a safe distance\'97Jamison Hardy\'92s old money coddled preferred treatment in all things. On the ground, Fisher had arranged for a connecting limo to take Chanta\'92s party the few blocks back.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Abe and Major stood as soon as they saw him, gobsmacked at his entourage. After much back slapping and exclamations of \'93look at you!\'94, Chanta made introductions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 \'93Abe and Major, meet Peggy, Nelly and Rusty.\'94\
\'93Rusty, huh? Not too Rusty, I hope, huh?\'94 Major joked.\
Rusty laughed, just enough to placate Major and little enough to let Chanta know she was not all that amused. \'93Oh not at all,\'94 she said. \'93Chanta makes sure we\'92re all tuned and well-maintained.\'94\
\'93Oh, I bet he does!\'94 Major said.\
\'93Won\'92t you join us?\'94 Abe asked. \'93I\'92ll just fetch a couple more chairs.\'94\
\'93Oh you have plenty,\'94 said Nelly. \'93So sit yourself down.\'94\
Abe and Major instantly became compliant and suggestible, and when Rusty had settled onto Chanta\'92s lap, and then Nelly on Abe\'92s, and Peggy on Major\'92s, both of them sat rigid and appeared to be holding their breaths, until Major stiffly reached for his beer and drew a breath.\
\'93I\'92ll just fetch us some drinks, then, shall I?\'94 Peggy offered, and was gone before anyone could answer.\
\'93I heard you were a rock star now,\'94 Abe said to Chanta.\
\'93I heard you were dead,\'94 said Major.\
\'93Which reminds me: pay up,\'94 Abe said and made a gimme-gimme-cash gesture at Major who reluctantly flicked a funds transfer from his tablet. Abe\'92s tablet beeped acceptance.\
\'93And I heard you came into an inheritance,\'94 Abe continued.\
\'93Life is comfortable,\'94 Chanta agreed, and the Elvisettes giggled.\
Abe repeated his gesture at Major who again settled the bet with a funds transfer. \'93Stop talking, Chanta. You\'92re sending me broke.\'94\
\'93Well, this one\'92s not broke,\'94 Nelly said as she wiggled on Abe\'92s lap.\
Abe could not decide where to put his hands, so clutched his beer glass in both and drained it.\
Peggy returned with beers, glasses and a bottle of sparkling chard/pinot. \'93They only had domestic,\'94 she complained as she settled onto Major\'92s lap.\
\'93They don\'92t serve cocktails?\'94 Rusty asked, as she poured the bubbly.\
\'93Not Elvi cocktails, sugar. I swear, I doubt they have a single jar of peanut butter in this bar.\'94  Peggy drank from her glass, neither a sip nor a gulp. \'93Or a rasher of bacon.\'94\
Nelly shrugged, which made Abe squirm. \'93Drink up,\'94 she said.\
\'93Here\'92s to the Rock Against Revival!\'94 Rusty toasted.\
They all joined in, Chanta somewhat less enthusiastically.\
The Elvisettes explained about the New Holy Roller sect who, in Peggy\'92s words, \'93were forbidden to have groupies\'94 and \'93were no fun any more\'94. Nelly told Abe and Major about how \'93the good Elvi\'94 learned about a long-dead guy named Clinton Dawkins (\'93the One\'94) and the Holy Momma\'92s unborn baby (\'93the One After the One\'94) who was a clone of the Original Dawkins (\'93the One\'94).\
\'93Are you keeping up?\'94 Nelly asked Major.\
\'93Barely.\'94\
\'93So now there are two religious factions of Elvi?\'94 Abe asked.\
\'93No, only one, silly,\'94 Peggy answered. \'93The Evolution Elvi\'97the good guys\'97avoid all that gospel stuff, and are eagerly awaiting the birth of Little Clinton\'97\'94\
\'93The One After the One,\'94 Nelly interjected.\
\'93\'97who will become our prophet in a new era of science and reason.\'94\
Chanta felt exhausted. \'93Any wonder I have writer\'92s block. I have to write songs about this mess.\'94\
\'93You wrote better songs when you lived in the Ghetto, Chanta,\'94 Rusty said.\
Chanta agreed. \'93These Holy Rollers don\'92t help. It\'92s like they are not Elvi at all any more.\'94 He told Abe and Major about what had become of The Memphis Mafia Bar & Grill, which they knew was Chanta\'92s favourite Elvi Bar, though they\'92d never been there, or to anywhere in the Ghetto, only a couple of postcodes away from where they sat drinking.\
Chanta finished, shaking his head. \'93I never thought I\'92d be caught in a church.\'94\
\'93You couldn\'92t walk out?\'94 Major asked.\
\'93You should speak up,\'94 Rusty told Chanta. \'93You\'92re the Great Teacher. They believe every word you say.\'94\
\'93Great Teacher, huh?\'94 Abe sounded impressed, and proud by association.\
\'93Not the Holy Rollers,\'94 Chanta said. \'93They\'92re too far gone. You can 
\f2\i see
\f1\i0  religion in their eyes.\'94\
\'93Cheer up, Chanta. Every Elvis I know will come see the show,\'94 Nelly said. \'93There\'92s hope yet.\'94\
\'93Yeah,\'94 Peggy said. \'93The Elvi can\'92t go on together, with religious minds.\'94
